ield/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332250}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00f3edb2-19bb-54fa-b405-1ba68902442b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49505" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/4)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$ \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22bf65d9-1368-5c83-bc06-2f787ae31aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330249} {:answer/id #uuid "b84605c3-6d64-59e4-a03f-63d799bb1439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331577} {:answer/id #uuid "c